WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.530
- [Narrator] The average U.S. household

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:05.563
consumes about 11,000 kilowatt
hours of electricity a year.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.820
The biggest share of that power

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:11.700
about 16% goes to this, air conditioning.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.470
Summer months, spring higher temperatures

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:15.380
and higher electricity bills.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.920
But a Wall Street Journal analysis

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:18.820
found you could be paying
more than your neighbors

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.110
to keep your house cool.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.150
And may not even know it,
even if the power is generated

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.140
in the same exact place and transmitted

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.951
on the same exact power lines.

00:00:26.951 --> 00:00:30.240
(power lines humming)

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.920
To understand how this happens,

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.140
first, you need to understand
how power gets to your house.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.893
Power plants generate electricity.

00:00:36.893 --> 00:00:39.320
The voltage is cranked up at a substation

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.160
and sent across long
distance transmission lines.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.940
Crank down again, then sent
through distribution lines

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.730
that connect to your home.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.670
In many cases, everything from
the plant to the power poles

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.350
are owned by a regulated utility company.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:53.700
They supply you with the power

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.110
and bill you for how much you use.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.330
Government officials let
the utilities charge enough

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.980
to cover costs and make a modest profit.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.250
But high electricity
prices cost regulators

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:05.750
to re-examine this model.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.450
- The big question that
drove a lot of thinking

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.350
in the 1980s around
electricity and deregulation

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:18.370
or restructuring, was did the whole system

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.710
have to be a monopoly?

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:23.400
Or could there be pieces of
the electricity ecosystem,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.440
parts of how electricity is
generated, moved over wires,

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.860
and then supply it ultimately to customers

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.940
that could be competitive
businesses and kind of broken off.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.160
- [Narrator] Deregulation
was in vogue from railroads

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.360
to telecommunications

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.670
and the energy market was no exception.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.510
It opened up to allow for
companies to compete to sell power

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.300
to homes and businesses.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.390
These new entities known
as retail energy companies

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.210
would buy electricity from generators

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.730
and sell it to consumers

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.670
usually over the local utilities wires.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.170
The argument for deregulation
went that giving consumers

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.730
a choice between their old utilities

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.150
and new arrivals would
create competitive pricing

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.940
and lower their bills.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:03.270
Eventually at least 18 States deregulated

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.210
retail residential electricity
to varying degrees,

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:07.220
but in many of them,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.940
the promised savings haven't materialized.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.780
According to a Wall
Street Journal analysis

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.330
between 2010 and 2019, U.S.
consumers who signed up

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:19.010
with retail energy companies
paid $19.2 billion more

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:20.430
than they would have if they'd stuck

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.570
with their incumbent utilities.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.120
Retail customers have
paid the most in Texas

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.040
which deregulated this
energy market the furthest.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.000
This system came under intense scrutiny

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.990
after a February, 2021 winter
storm crippled its power grid

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.260
and left many Texas
residents with electric bills

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:38.876
that soared into the thousands of dollars.

00:02:38.876 --> 00:02:41.900
Electricity deregulation
has worked for business.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.400
Federal data show it led
to substantial savings

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.800
for commercial and
industrial power customers

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.430
whose large scale electricity use

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.350
give them the incentive to shop around

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:52.492
and seek expert guidance.

00:02:52.492 --> 00:02:54.920
The retail power industry
largely languished

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.910
until about a decade ago.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.750
When many states adopted a regulation

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.700
called purchase of receivables

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.710
that made the traditional
utility companies

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.350
solely responsible for unpaid bills.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.360
Experts have said that
because they were no longer

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.160
responsible for unpaid bills

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.480
retail power companies, didn't
have to worry about the risk

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.210
of signing up lower income consumers.

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.600
- There are often many, many variations

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:22.370
in the kind of electricity
contract that you might sign

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.100
with a retail provider.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.530
Just as for cell phone contracts

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:29.220
you might be negotiating
about data and minutes

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.910
and other bells and whistles
that might come with a contract

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:35.930
that might increase its attractiveness.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.350
A second really important similarity

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.720
has to do with the sort of central role

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.160
that getting customers
plays in the business model

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.330
of retail electricity providers

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.390
- [Narrator] Experts say
retail energy salespeople

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:51.480
often go door to door and offer perks

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.790
for switching to their
service such as gift cards

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.380
or free light bulbs.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.640
Many states have
investigated retail providers

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:00.800
marketing tactics that target
elderly and poor customers,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.500
as well as heavily minority neighborhoods

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.110
where electricity bills account
for larger shares of income.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.717
A 2018 Illinois lawsuits
said "An energy retailer

00:04:08.717 --> 00:04:11.407
"carried out a particularly
egregious marketing scheme

00:04:11.407 --> 00:04:14.207
"that disproportionately impacted
African-American consumers

00:04:14.207 --> 00:04:16.380
"on the South and West side of Chicago."

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.180
And an analysis of the
Connecticut retail power industry

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.980
revealed that retail power customers

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:23.461
receiving state assistance
to cover electricity costs

00:04:23.461 --> 00:04:26.160
known as hardship customers paid premiums

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.440
that were on average nearly
50% higher than other customers

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.140
for retail providers.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.210
Susan Baldwin an independent
consultant for public utilities

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.710
and consumer advocates
says some retail companies

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:38.470
have questionable marketing tactics

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.620
with aggressive sales pitches
aimed at enrolling customers.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.909
- It shows disproportionate harm

00:04:43.909 --> 00:04:47.350
to those who can least afford to pay

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.120
for an essential service,
this is not a luxury item.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.270
This is electricity.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:52.620
- [Narrator] Part of the problem is that

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.600
many consumers don't know who
is selling them their power.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.970
In most states that allow
competitive electricity markets

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:00.490
for residential customers,
bills are provided

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.100
by the incumbent utility company

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.330
even if the power is delivered
by a retail supplier.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.250
The identity of the retailer is disclosed

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.300
somewhere on the bill, but
it isn't always obvious.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.330
Here's one way to check if
you're paying more for power.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.510
Look for the part of the bill,

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:16.791
that details price comparison information.

00:05:16.791 --> 00:05:18.380
There you should find the price

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.920
for the so-called standard offer service

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.530
or SOS for one kilowatt
hour of electricity.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:27.453
That is the average rate charged
by the incumbent utility.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.140
Next, look for how much your
electric supplier charged

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.590
for that billing period

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.493
which is often reported alongside
the name of the company.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:38.830
Take the rate your
electric supplier charged

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.710
and multiply it by the
number of kilowatt hours used

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:44.213
to get the amount paid
to the retail supplier.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.120
To figure out the difference
between what you paid

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.470
to the supplier and what you
would've paid to your utility,

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.520
multiply the SOS rate by
the kilowatt hours used

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.570
and subtract that amount

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.603
from the amount paid to the supplier.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:00.360
The retail, companies can see

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.060
that there has been some
bad behavior in the market

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.410
and that the industry needs
to improve its reputation.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.580
An industry backers say
residential customers

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:11.520
need better education from
the industry and regulators

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.800
on how the retail electricity market works

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.870
and how it can benefit them.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.680
Consumers can take advantage
of the deregulated marketplace

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.010
by strategically shifting

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.060
to suppliers with the lowest prices.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:25.300
But that can require reading
their contracts fine print.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.530
Consumer advocates also
encourage customers

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.450
to scrutinize their bill and report

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.330
any potential wrongdoing.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.100
And consumers can always switch back

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:35.470
to their incumbent utility provider.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:37.550
- If you look at your bill,

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:39.610
and it seems to be through the roof,

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:42.410
and it's not what you were led to believe

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:46.140
you were told one thing about
rates being wonderfully low

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.850
and they ended up being terribly high,

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:54.850
call your public utility
commission and complain.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:55.703
- [Narrator] Retail power

00:06:55.703 --> 00:06:57.130
was supposed to be a light bulb moment

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.700
for the energy industry,

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:00.820
but the cost of keeping that light bulb on

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.637
appear to be higher than they could be.

00:07:03.637 --> 00:07:06.036
(fading low music)

